a conclusion. I'm keeping the length to roughly 2,700 words and using a short reference list.\n\nI'm making sure the quotes I pull from the source match its wording, especially the line on subjunctive dependencies and the one calling their structure the largest open problem, and I'll keep any quoted fragments short.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm planning a roughly 2,400–3,000 word essay on A-FDT, built around Equation (4) as the main formal presentation. The plan is to use the name A-FDT throughout, and to give any other decision theory I mention its own distinct label, such as CDT, EDT, or an FDT' variant.\n\nI'm now settling on the naming: the paper's general idea stays FDT, but the Equation (4) version gets labeled A-FDT, while CDT, EDT, the FDT′ variant, the evidential version, UDT and TDT keep their own names. I'm also noting that the formula is argmax over the expected value of the do-intervention on the fdt node, with observations handled by changing which node gets intervened on rather than by conditioning.\n\nI'm checking the transparent Newcomb calculation next, since the source's notation for conditioning on the observation is inconsistent with Equation (4), which takes no conditioning term. Working through the one-box case, I get an accurate-prediction branch worth $1,000,000 and an inaccurate branch worth $1000q, which totals 990,000 + 10q.\n\nI'm checking the two-box total: the accurate-prediction branch gives about $10,010 and the inaccurate branch adds $990q, so it comes to roughly $10,010 + $990q, not the $110,000 the source prints. Working through both one-box and two-box totals confirms one-boxing at 990,000 + 10q beats two-boxing at 11,000 for any q.\n\nI'm checking the blackmail numbers: with a 0.09% error rate, refusing costs about $900 in expectation, which is less than paying $1,000, so the break-even sits near an error rate of 0.1%. The source's second example looks off, since 0.011% would only cost $110, so the claim that the agent pays there doesn't hold, and it probably meant 1.1%.\n\nI'm working through the smoking lesion case next, where the expected-utility gap between the options comes down to a probability times $1,000, so the difference stays small and simple. For the blackmail example, I'm noting the second figure looks like it should be 1.1% rather than 0.011%, since at 0.011% the expected loss from refusing is only about $110.\n\nI'm checking the Death in Damascus case under A-FDT: staying costs nothing, while fleeing to Aleppo ends in death with a $1,000 loss, so staying wins. For the twin prisoner's dilemma, forcing both copies to cooperate gives the best payoff. I'm also working through the Parfit's hitchhiker case, where paying the driver's fee gives an expected value of about -$990 once the 1% chance of being left behind is counted.\n\nI'm finishing the Parfit's hitchhiker comparison: paying gives about -$10,990 once the chance of being left behind is counted, while refusing gives about -$990,000, so paying comes out ahead. I'm treating that calculation as my own modeling, since the source doesn't work it out. Next I'm listing the open problems the essay should raise: how to construct the world model G, counterpossibles, fairness, policies versus actions, self-reference, and variable ordering when the agent's own algorithm isn't known.\n\nI'm weighing whether the observation should be treated as a hard condition in the transparent Newcomb calculation, since the source writes \"Obs=full\" there while Equation (4) takes no conditioning term. I'm leaning toward reading the observation as only the argument of the intervened node, with its value in the hypothetical fixed by the graph, and flagging the source's notation as ambiguous on this point.\n\nI'm settling how the observation enters Equation (4): it's only the argument of the intervened node, with its value in the hypothetical set by the graph, not a condition I impose. Other nodes for indices I'm not intervening on still need priors, and the cleanest way to handle that is to compute the outputs themselves recursively, policy by policy.\n\nI'm sketching the essay's structure: an introduction that settles the naming, then the setup in expected-utility form, then Equation (4) and its parts. Next comes subjunctive dependence, and I'm also checking whether a policy-level version of the transparent Newcomb calculation reproduces the same 990,010 figure without needing priors on the other observation branches.\n\nI'm now laying out the essay's sections: the graph setup, the worked examples including Parfit's hitchhiker, a short comparison with CDT and EDT, the open problems with my operational recipe, and the global-perspective motivation before a conclusion. I'm keeping the length to roughly 2,700 words and using a short reference list.\n\nI'm making sure the quotes I pull from the source match its wording, especially the line on subjunctive dependencies and the one calling their structure the largest open problem, and I'll keep any quoted fragments short.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":83920,"completion_tokens":11088,"total_tokens":95008,"cost":0.27872,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.27872,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.16784,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.11088},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3340,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}